A matched pair takes the size its content's core needs, and its scripts grow it by one size at most, when their reach is nearer the next size: $(n^2)$, $(x_i^2 + y_i^2)$, $(e^(x^2))$ and $((x+1)^2)$ take the next size, not the one after; $(2^n)$, $(x_i)$ and $O(n log n)$ keep theirs, and so does $(f'(x))$ — a prime is the symbol's. A fraction grows them, $(a/b)$ and $lfloor x/2 rfloor$; `lr` covers the scripts too, $lr((, n^2, ))$.

$ (sum_(i=1)^n x_i^2) = (n (n+1))/2 $
